import re
p = r'.\discussion_source.txt'
s = open(p, encoding='utf-8').read(); paras = s.strip().split('\n\n'); print(len(paras))
def rep(i, old, new):
    assert paras[i].count(old) == 1, (i, old[:50], paras[i].count(old)); paras[i] = paras[i].replace(old, new)
# paragraph 1
paras[0] = ("The radar analysis gave a coherent picture of where the Deyr season flood of late 2023 left its signal around Beledweyne. Flagged area concentrated along the river corridor, fell mainly on cropland and on pixels mapped as built-up, and agreed only partly with the optical flood mapping available for the town, with confident detections but many missed flooded pixels. The flagged total also moved substantially with analyst choices, and the built-up exposure rests on a map class that independent building data support only in part. These sensitivities condition how every area in the Results should be read.")
# paragraph 2, built-up sentence
rep(1, "The high proportion in built-up land here probably reflects the position of the town inside the floodplain together with the small mapped extent of that class, and it should not be extended to settlements elsewhere.",
    "The high proportion in the built-up class should not be read as the exposure of settlements. Most of the mapped built-up area lay far from any building in an independent footprint dataset, the flagged share was lower near buildings than away from them, and only a minority of buildings had a flagged centroid pixel, a count that is itself a lower bound because the rule misses much of the built-up surface. The class therefore appears to include surfaces within the floodplain that two land-cover products both labelled as built-up but that are not buildings, although this was not examined and the footprint dataset may omit structures. The class proportion should not be extended to settlements elsewhere.")
# paragraph 3 table reference
rep(2, "(Table 9)", "(Table 6)")
# paragraph 4, pre-event reference
paras[3] = ("The strong dependence of the flagged area on the pre-event reference needs separate comment. Backscatter varies between acquisition dates for reasons unrelated to flooding, and over farmland and built surfaces monthly and orbit-related differences can exceed the contrast produced by inundation, as found in one Chinese catchment, and not every land-cover change between dates is flood-induced <<10.3390/rs14153838;10.3390/rs14020246>>. The diagnostics point to the 9 October scene rather than the 21 October scene as the one that differed. The 21 October and 2 November scenes were close to each other in backscatter, whereas the 9 October scene was darker by a similar amount in every land-cover class, including rangeland where little flooding was flagged, and the footprints obtained with the three references were nested. Most of the extra area flagged with the 21 October reference lay in vegetation or rangeland. Rainfall estimates show no rain in the 3 days before 9 October and substantial rain in the 3 days before 21 October and 2 November, so the 9 October scene may have been acquired over drier soil and vegetation than the later scenes, which would raise backscatter in the later scenes and enlarge the decreases measured against them. Sentinel-1 backscatter is used to retrieve surface soil moisture, so such an effect is plausible <<10.1109/TGRS.2018.2858004>>, but it was not tested because no soil moisture data were used and the rainfall product is coarse. The diagnostics also do not show which reference is correct for flood detection, since the references that included the later scenes agreed better with the independent mappings in the town and worse with the calibration mapping. A mean of three scenes behaved much like the mean of two, so adding one scene did not settle the question, although a multi-date reference remains a more stable design than a single scene. The same variability may explain why thresholds taken unchanged from other settings flagged a far larger area at much lower precision, whereas thresholds fitted to local optical mapping did not, since established mapping chains typically derive their thresholds from the imagery itself and pair them with filters or context layers that flag likely false positives <<10.1016/j.rse.2022.113273;10.1016/j.rse.2025.115108>>. A cluster filter served that second purpose here, since small scattered clusters made up most of the flagged pixels in the rangeland boxes that were inspected. The improvement in agreement shows that the removed clusters were on balance less often flooded, not that all of them were false, because the removed pixels were flooded less often than the scoring masks as a whole but not never, and clusters of 50 to 99 pixels were flooded about as often as the surrounding mask. The filter therefore probably removes some genuine small inundation, and the exposure estimates for fragmented flooding are probably underestimates. Most of the area it removed lay in rangeland, where no reference exists to test the choice. Terrain masks, by contrast, changed the footprint little, which means that almost no flagged area lay on steep or elevated ground, whereas in the complex terrain of the Awash basin masks based on height above nearest drainage and slope removed a substantial share of overestimation <<10.1038/s41598-026-63431-0;10.1016/j.rse.2022.113273>>.")
# paragraph 6 constraints
paras[5] = ("Several constraints limit what these results can support. The reference flood extents came from optical imagery acquired two to four days away from the radar dates, they are not field observations, and they cover only the town. The three products together covered a small part of the study area, and almost all of the analysed vegetation or rangeland, which makes up about two thirds of the analysed area, lay outside every reference extent, so accuracy there remains untested and the rule has not been validated across the whole study area. Accuracy figures from the town cannot be carried over to the full-area totals, and the flood statistics refer to the analysed area and not to the whole rectangle. The land-cover map was trained on labels where two existing products agreed, not on field data, and its built-up class had only 4 hold-out points because of the spatial block split. Independent building footprints showed that much of the mapped built-up area contains few or no buildings, so exposure shares for built-up land are indicative only and probably overstate the exposure of settlements. The flood rule was calibrated on one date, two later refinements were introduced after the independent dates had already been scored once, and the terrain limits came from the same town pixels, all of which may flatter the reported agreement. A single orbit with a 12-day repeat could not resolve the flood crest, so the date of largest flagged area need not be the date of peak inundation, and the analysis contains no gauge, depth, soil moisture or population data. The difference between totals from the two October reference scenes is only partly explained, and the area over the town extent fell from 51.2 km² to 45.7 km² when the evaluation grid was coarsened from 10 m to 20 m, which was not caused by counting pixels on the coarser grid, because averaging the 10 m flag layer to 20 m left the area unchanged, but followed the 5 by 5 pixel median window, which spans 100 m and not 50 m on the coarser grid, since the 10 m rule gave 45.6 km² with a 9 by 9 pixel window. Totals therefore depend on the ground size of the filter window and on other processing details that few readers will see.")
# paragraph 7, operational
rep(6, "Until then, flagged area in vegetated and built-up land is best treated as a lower bound, whereas the cropland footprint is likely the more reliable part of the map.", "Until then, flagged area in built-up pixels near buildings is best treated as a lower bound because recall there was low, whereas the cropland footprint is likely the more reliable part of the map.")
rep(6, "Class-level exposure summaries of the kind reported here give planners a way to relate radar-derived footprints to cropland and settlements, building on susceptibility assessments for the basin and on indicator frameworks developed in other floodplains",
    "Class-level exposure summaries of the kind reported here give planners a way to relate radar-derived footprints to cropland, building on susceptibility assessments for the basin and on indicator frameworks developed in other floodplains")
paras[6] = paras[6].rstrip() + " Exposure of settlements is better estimated from building footprints than from a built-up class, as the comparison with the footprint dataset suggests."
paras[7] = ("The main contribution of the study is a documented radar workflow that pairs a locally calibrated rule with validation against independent optical mapping and with an explicit account of how far the answer moves with analyst choices. Its message is deliberately modest. The flood footprint appears credible where inundation was extensive and open, cropland is the exposed class mapped most reliably, and the totals should be treated as conditional on analytic choices until gauge, rainfall and field observations allow them to be anchored.")
open(p, 'w', encoding='utf-8', newline='').write('\n\n'.join(paras) + '\n')
txt = '\n\n'.join(paras); print([c for c in ':;\u2014\u2013' if c in re.sub(r'<<[^>]+>>', '', txt)], len(txt.split()))
